\section{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Statistical power and operating point confounds.}
Only DeepSeek-R1-8B shows HS probes Pareto-dominating text probes across all metrics ($p = 0.044$ for FPR, not Bonferroni-corrected). On 32B models, HS probes show directional accuracy advantages (+8.4pp on R1-32B, $p{=}0.080$; +0.9pp on QwQ-32B, $p{=}0.828$) but neither survives Bonferroni correction, making it impossible to declare Pareto dominance for either modality. Each model uses a different optimal window size ($W{=}15, 10, 5$ respectively), further complicating cross-model modality comparisons. With 43 test traces for R1-8B and approximately 20 for each 32B model, statistical power is limited. With only ${\sim}20$ test traces per 32B model, our bootstrap tests lack power to detect moderate effect sizes (Cohen's $d < 0.5$), meaning the non-significant results ($p{=}0.080$, $p{=}0.828$) cannot be interpreted as evidence of absence. Larger evaluation sets with matched temporal conditions are needed to determine whether the R1-8B dominance pattern generalizes.

\paragraph{Correlation, not causation.}
Our probing methodology establishes that safety commitment is \emph{decodable} from intermediate hidden states. It does not demonstrate that these representations causally drive the model's safe or unsafe behavior. Causal intervention studies, such as activation patching at the identified commitment point, would be necessary to verify that the probed features are mechanistically load-bearing rather than epiphenomenal.

\paragraph{Commitment point definition.}
The commitment point (CP) annotation relies on detecting the presence or absence of explicit safety reasoning in the chain-of-thought. Edge cases exist where models oscillate between safe and unsafe reasoning within a single trace, or embed safety-relevant considerations inside otherwise harmful text. We have not yet conducted inter-annotator agreement studies or sensitivity analyses over alternative CP definitions; these remain important directions for future work.

\paragraph{Dataset scale and diversity.}
Models are evaluated on 95--209 traces drawn from the HarmThoughts dataset (209 for R1-8B, 100 for R1-32B, 95 for QwQ-32B), covering a specific set of jailbreak categories. We have not tested generalization to other attack types (e.g., multi-turn persuasion, encoded prompts), other languages, or substantially different prompt formats. Broader evaluation is needed before drawing conclusions about the universality of the commitment point phenomenon.

\paragraph{High false positive rate on safe traces.}
All probing methods produce false positive rates between 39\% and 62\% when applied to entirely safe traces. This level of specificity makes the current probes suitable as diagnostic research tools but unsuitable for deployment as production safety monitors without substantial improvements in precision.

\paragraph{Limited architectural diversity.}
Two of the three tested models (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B) share the Qwen-2.5 architecture. The sole Llama-based model (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B) is also the only one exhibiting a clear HS probing advantage. This confound makes it difficult to disentangle whether the observed differences arise from architecture, model scale, or distillation procedure. Evaluation on a wider range of base architectures (e.g., Mistral, Gemma, LLaMA-3) is needed to resolve this ambiguity.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate contrastive trace probes across three reasoning models (R1-8B, R1-32B, QwQ-32B), two signal modalities (hidden states and text), and multiple temporal aggregation strategies. Our experiments address four questions: (1) How much does temporal context improve detection? (2) Does the choice of signal modality matter? (3) Can sequence models detect unsafe reasoning earlier? (4) Do probes transfer across models?

\subsection{Temporal Context as Primary Factor}
\label{sec:temporal_primary}

The most consistent finding across all experimental conditions is that temporal context improves probe precision and reduces FPR, regardless of signal modality. \cref{fig:window_ablation} shows this pattern for R1-8B; precision and FPR gains hold across all three models, though balanced accuracy improvement is model-dependent.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_temporal_improvement.pdf}
\caption{Effect of temporal window size on R1-8B HS probe performance. Precision increases monotonically from 69.3\% ($W{=}1$) to 82.2\% ($W{=}25$); FPR decreases from 9.65\% to 4.83\%. Coverage decreases as larger windows make the probe more conservative.}
\label{fig:window_ablation}
\end{figure}

On R1-8B, increasing window size from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$ improves balanced accuracy from 77.8\% to 81.0\% (+3.2pp), precision from 69.3\% to 82.2\% (+12.9pp), and reduces step-level FPR from 9.65\% to 4.83\% ($-$4.8pp). QwQ-32B shows a similar monotonic pattern (+3.2pp accuracy, +7.4pp precision, $-$5.9pp FPR from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$). R1-32B exhibits a non-monotonic trajectory: accuracy peaks at $W{=}10$ then declines below the $W{=}1$ baseline by $W{=}25$ (71.8\% $\rightarrow$ 70.3\%), though precision and FPR still improve (+1.7pp, $-$5.5pp). Across all three models, FPR consistently decreases with larger windows (\cref{tab:temporal_main}). Larger windows make probes more conservative: fewer traces cross the detection threshold (coverage decreases from 72.1\% to 48.8\% on R1-8B under $K{=}1$ criterion), but those that do are classified with higher confidence.

The coverage-quality trade-off depends on the detection criterion. Under $K{=}1$ (single threshold crossing), larger $W$ reduces coverage because the smoothed probe score crosses the boundary fewer times. Under stricter criteria ($K{=}5$, requiring consecutive crossings), larger $W$ can improve effective coverage by eliminating transient false crossings. We report $K{=}1$ results throughout for interpretability, noting that deployment systems would tune $K$ alongside $W$.

The key observation is that the magnitude of temporal improvement on precision (+1.7 to +12.9pp) and FPR ($-$4.8 to $-$5.9pp) dwarfs the modality gap (0 to 7pp FPR difference in the strongest case). Temporal context is modality-agnostic: whether probes read hidden activations or generated text, observing multiple reasoning steps provides the dominant source of discriminative information for FPR reduction.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & $W$ & \textbf{Bal.\ Acc.} & \textbf{Precision} & \textbf{FPR} \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{R1-8B} & 1 & 77.8 & 69.3 & 9.65 \\
 & 15 & 80.2 & 77.7 & 6.39 \\
 & 25 & \textbf{81.0} & \textbf{82.2} & \textbf{4.83} \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{R1-32B} & 1 & 71.8 & 54.9 & 32.0 \\
 & 10 & \textbf{73.7} & \textbf{60.9} & \textbf{23.4} \\
 & 25 & 70.3 & 56.6 & 26.5 \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{QwQ-32B} & 1 & 73.0 & 54.4 & 22.8 \\
 & 5 & 76.1 & 57.3 & 21.7 \\
 & 25 & \textbf{76.2} & \textbf{61.8} & \textbf{16.9} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Temporal windowing results (HS probes). Metrics are step-level (each reasoning step treated independently). Precision and FPR improve consistently across all models as $W$ increases. Balanced accuracy peaks at $W{=}25$ for R1-8B and QwQ-32B but at $W{=}10$ for R1-32B.}
\label{tab:temporal_main}
\end{table}

\subsection{Hidden States vs. Text: Model-Dependent Effects}
\label{sec:hs_vs_text}

Given the dominance of temporal context, we ask whether signal modality produces a consistent residual effect. \cref{tab:hs_vs_text} reports trace-level accuracy, precision, and pre-commitment FPR for HS and text probes across all three models. Trace-level FPR is the mean per-trace proportion of pre-CP steps exceeding the detection threshold, treating each trace as an independent observation to enable paired statistical comparison. Each model uses its validation-selected temporal window ($W{=}15$ for R1-8B, $W{=}10$ for R1-32B, $W{=}5$ for QwQ-32B), so cross-model comparisons reflect model-specific optima rather than a uniform condition.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Modality} & \textbf{Bal.\ Acc.} & \textbf{Precision} & \textbf{FPR} \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{R1-8B} & HS & \textbf{81.3} & \textbf{76.8} & \textbf{14.8} \\
 & Text & 73.1 & 54.4 & 22.2 \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{R1-32B} & HS & 79.4 & 61.0 & \textbf{24.2} \\
 & Text & 71.1 & 51.6 & 32.6 \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{QwQ-32B} & HS & 67.9 & 58.3 & 20.8 \\
 & Text & 67.1 & 52.5 & \textbf{17.8} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{HS vs.\ text probes at each model's validation-selected window ($W{=}15$ for R1-8B, $W{=}10$ for R1-32B, $W{=}5$ for QwQ-32B). Metrics are trace-level averages (each trace contributes one aggregated value). R1-8B shows clear HS dominance; 32B models show different precision-FPR tradeoff points.}
\label{tab:hs_vs_text}
\end{table}

On R1-8B ($n{=}43$), HS probes Pareto-dominate text probes with strong statistical support: higher precision (76.8\% vs.\ 54.4\%, +22.4pp, $p{<}0.001$), higher accuracy (81.3\% vs.\ 73.1\%, +8.2pp, $p{=}0.005$), and lower FPR (14.8\% vs.\ 22.2\%, $\Delta{=}{-}7.4$pp, $p{=}0.044$). Both precision and accuracy differences survive Bonferroni correction for three model comparisons ($\alpha{=}0.017$); the FPR difference does not. Precision comparisons exclude traces where neither probe produced a positive prediction (10/43 on R1-8B), as precision is undefined in this case; the bootstrap operates on the remaining 33 traces.\footnote{Excluding undefined-precision traces is conservative: these traces represent cases where both probes correctly abstain, and including them would not change the direction of the effect.} This is a genuine dominance result where HS probes improve on every metric simultaneously, with the two strongest effects confirmed at high statistical confidence.

The 32B models present a different picture. On R1-32B ($n{=}20$), HS probes show directional advantages in precision (+9.4pp, $p{=}0.050$) and accuracy (+8.4pp, $p{=}0.080$), with neither surviving Bonferroni correction ($\alpha{=}0.017$). The effect sizes parallel the R1-8B pattern, but the smaller test set limits statistical power. On QwQ-32B ($n{=}19$), differences are negligible: precision +5.7pp ($p{=}0.346$), accuracy +0.9pp ($p{=}0.828$). The modality advantage is thus model-dependent: HS probes clearly dominate on R1-8B, show a directional but underpowered advantage on R1-32B, and offer no measurable benefit on QwQ-32B.

\cref{fig:model_dependency} visualizes the trace-level FPR comparison. R1-8B shows a clear HS advantage (14.8\% vs.\ 22.2\%, $\Delta{=}{-}7.4$pp); R1-32B shows a larger gap favoring HS (24.2\% vs.\ 32.6\%) but without statistical significance after correction; QwQ-32B shows a reversed pattern where text achieves marginally lower FPR (17.8\% vs.\ 20.8\%, n.s.).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_model_dependency.pdf}
\caption{Trace-level pre-CP FPR by modality and model with bootstrap 95\% CIs. R1-8B shows a significant HS advantage (14.8\% vs.\ 22.2\%, $p{=}0.044$); R1-32B and QwQ-32B differences are not significant.}
\label{fig:model_dependency}
\end{figure}

The practical implication is that modality choice is model-dependent. On R1-8B, HS probes are clearly preferred. On 32B models, the choice depends on whether the deployment scenario prioritizes precision (favoring HS) or low FPR (favoring text). Per-model validation is necessary rather than blanket modality assumptions.

\subsection{GRU Sequence Models and Detection Timing}
\label{sec:gru}

GRU classifiers trained on full step sequences test whether temporal dependencies beyond a fixed window improve early detection. The best result (R1-8B multi-layer GRU) achieves +4.5$\pm$3.0 steps lead with 72.9\% crossing accuracy; QwQ-32B achieves +15.9 steps lead with 84.0\% crossing accuracy. R1-32B shows weaker performance (crossing accuracy 23--30\%). Detection timing varies by modality on R1-8B (HS detects at the commitment point with low FPR; text detects ${\sim}$10 steps earlier with higher FPR), but the two modalities show comparable timing on 32B models. Full GRU results and per-seed breakdowns are in \cref{app:gru_details}.

\subsection{Control Task: Signal Validation}
\label{sec:control}

A probe that achieves high accuracy on structured labels might simply exploit positional information (e.g., unsafe steps tend to occur later in a trace). Following \cite{hewitt2019control}, we train control probes on randomly permuted labels to measure selectivity: the gap between linguistic probe accuracy and control probe accuracy.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Probe Acc.} & \textbf{Selectivity} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & 14 & 81.7 & 12.5pp \\
QwQ-32B & 2 & 67.8 & 15.3pp \\
R1-32B & 10 & 58.3 & 7.5pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Control task selectivity (probe accuracy minus control probe accuracy on random labels). All models exceed the 5pp threshold from \citet{hewitt2019control}, confirming genuine safety signal.}
\label{tab:control}
\end{table}

\cref{tab:control} reports selectivity scores: R1-8B 12.5pp (layer 14), QwQ-32B 15.3pp (layer 2), R1-32B 7.5pp (layer 10). All three models exceed the 5pp threshold typically considered meaningful, confirming that probes exploit genuine safety-related structure in the representations rather than positional artifacts alone.

As an additional validation, we compare the HS probe FPR against a position-only baseline that predicts ``unsafe'' based solely on step index. On R1-8B, the probe clearly outperforms the position baseline (FPR 8.1\% vs.\ 15.8\%), confirming genuine safety encoding. On QwQ-32B (layer 2), the position baseline accuracy (70.6\%) slightly exceeds the HS probe accuracy (67.8\%), and on R1-32B (layer 10) selectivity is near the threshold at 7.5pp. These results suggest that 32B model probes at their selected layers encode safety state partially through positional correlations, though selectivity still exceeds the 5pp criterion on all models.

\subsection{Negative Results: Change-Point and Geometric Methods}
\label{sec:negative}

\cref{tab:negative} evaluates change-point and geometric methods. BOCPD ($\lambda{=}10$) achieves 93\% detection rate (vs.\ 65.1\% for simple thresholding) but with worse lead time (+3.5 vs.\ +4.9 steps), revealing a coverage-timing tradeoff rather than an early detection advantage. All CUSUM configurations underperform simple thresholding. Mahalanobis distances (TrajGuard methodology) are indistinguishable between safe and jailbreak trajectories ($d{=}6.64$--$6.73$ on R1-8B), rendering geometric methods ineffective. The transition from safe to unsafe is gradual rather than abrupt, explaining why change-point methods designed for regime shifts fail to provide early warning. Full configuration sweeps are in \cref{app:bocpd_config}.

